  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(2\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1}=\\frac{8\\times 3}{3\\times 1}=\\frac{24}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{3}=\\frac{24÷3}{3÷3}=\\frac{8}{1}=8\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
